<h2>The rule</h2>
<p>The bar was a hundred thousand living bearers and a population that is ninety percent fifty-plus. 34 names cleared it, out of the 533 names big enough to be considered.</p>
<p>Every one is a girls'' name. 34 of 34. Zero boys'' names made the list.</p>
<p>The average name on it has 311,704 living bearers and a median age of 70.7. Across the whole group, 93.9 percent of the people who answer to these names are fifty or older.</p>
<h2>One generation, packed into one list</h2>
<p>These names did not get old on their own schedule. They peaked together.</p>
<p>17 of the 34 peaked in the 1950s. 8 peaked in the 1940s. 3 peaked in the 1960s. 6 peaked before 1940.</p>
<p>The oldest summit belongs to <a href="/name/Dorothy/">Dorothy</a>, who peaked in 1924. The newest belongs to <a href="/name/Rhonda/">Rhonda</a>: 1965. Rhonda is also the youngest name on the list, and its median age is 62.</p>
<p>The most recent peak in this group is 61 years old.</p>
<h2>The biggest one</h2>
<p><a href="/name/Linda/">Linda</a> is the largest living population here: 1,010,533 people, median age 72. Its peak of 99,692 came in 1947.</p>
<p>6.8 of every thousand living Lindas is a child. The rest grew up. In 2025, 294 girls were named Linda.</p>
<h2>Where the children went</h2>
<p><a href="/name/Debra/">Debra</a> peaked at 50,563 births in 1955. A Debra born that year is 71 now.</p>
<p>445,808 living Debras. 1.9 of every thousand of them is under eighteen. Last year, 31 babies were named Debra.</p>
